    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": " The Jacobian matrix captures how a function changes in different directions. It tells you how much the function stretches or compresses space in each direction. For example, if the Jacobian determinant is positive, the function preserves orientation; if negative, it reverses orientation. It’s like measuring how much the function distorts space locally."
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Here is my question. \nCould you give me a version based only on the general impression? 🐈‍⬛✨"
